// Type-check `src/hosted`, which the local build deliberately excludes.
//
// WHY THIS DIRECTORY EXISTS AT ALL
//
// `src/hosted` is code for a *different deployment of this engine*: a server
// that connects mailboxes through a third party (Composio) and fetches mail on
// the user's behalf. The local app cannot contain it. Not "should not" —
// cannot: the product's one load-bearing claim is that mail never leaves the
// machine, and an account served this way breaks it silently, per account,
// while every surface still says otherwise.
//
// So it is kept, and it is kept *out*. `tsconfig.json` excludes it, so nothing
// here reaches `dist/`, the npm tarball, or the `.app`. This config is what
// stops "excluded" from decaying into "unchecked": code nobody compiles is code
// that quietly stops working, and discovering that on the day the hosted host
// is built is discovering it at the worst possible moment.
//
// The two overrides are the same enforcement `tsconfig.core.json` uses, for the
// same reason and against a stricter target:
//
//   "types": []          no @types/node, so `node:fs` does not resolve
//   "lib": WebWorker     a V8 isolate's ambient surface — no `process`, no
//                        `Buffer`, no `__dirname`
//
// The target is a Cloudflare Worker. It has no filesystem to save an attachment
// to and no OS credential store to read an API key from, which is why
// `ComposioConfig` is injected rather than looked up. Both mistakes typecheck
// perfectly under the root config; neither survives this one.
//
// Tests are excluded here because they run in Node (`node --test`) and use
// `node:test` / `node:assert`, which `types: []` cannot see. They are checked
// by `tsconfig.test.json`, which includes all of `src`.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "lib": ["ES2022", "WebWorker"],
    "types": [],
    "noEmit": true
  },
  "include": ["src/hosted", "src/core"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "dist", "src/**/*.test.ts"]
}
